<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place</p><h1 class="enc-title">Brazos Reserve</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1854">1854</a>–<a href="/years/1859">1859</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Brazos Indian Reservation</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Brazos Reserve was a reservation set up by the State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> in 1854–55 on the Brazos River in north-central Texas for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caddo">Caddo</a>, Anadarko, Waco, Tonkawa and other peoples. Hostility and attacks from white settlers led to its closure in 1859, when its inhabitants were moved north to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-territory">Indian Territory</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a>.</p></div>
